Null-pointer dereference when `experimentalReactComponentMeta` is true and `findMatchingComponent` returns `undefined`. The early-return guard is `if (!experimentalReactComponentMeta && !hasDocgen)`, so when the feature flag is on it does **not** fire even when `component` is `undefined`. The very next line, `const docgenResult = component.reactDocgen`, will throw a `TypeError` at runtime.

Fix: add an explicit guard that applies regardless of the feature flag, for example:
```typescript
if (!component) {
  return { manifest: { ...base, error: { name: 'No component found', message: '...' } } };
}
```
or add the `!component` condition to the existing early-return check.

`this.commandLine.fileNames.includes(fileName)` inside a `for` loop over `fileNames` is O(n) per call, making `ensureFiles` O(n²) overall. This method is called from `extractPropsFromStories` with every story batch; in a large design system (e.g. Mantine with 133 components) the quadratic scan could become noticeable.

Use a `Set` for the existence check:
```typescript
const existing = new Set(this.commandLine.fileNames);
for (const fileName of fileNames) {
  if (!existing.has(fileName)) {
    this.commandLine.fileNames.push(fileName);
    existing.add(fileName);
    added = true;
  }
}
```
